<span class="section-num">06</span>
<span class="eyebrow">FAQ</span>
<h2>What buyers still want to know</h2>
</div>
<div>
<div class="faq-list"><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>What barrel size should most people choose?</summary><p class="faq-answer">A 1.25-inch barrel is the safest default if you want loose curls or polished waves. Choose 1 inch if you want more defined curls, and 1.5 inches only if you mainly want soft waves or blowout shape.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>How hot should a curling iron actually be?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Most people do not need the highest setting. Fine or damaged hair should start low, often around 250–325°F, while thicker or more resistant hair may need more heat. The goal is the lowest temperature that forms a curl without repeated passes.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Is a long barrel worth it if my hair is not very long?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Sometimes. A longer barrel can make wrapping easier even on medium-length hair, but it matters most when hair is long enough to crowd a standard barrel. If your sections already wrap cleanly, barrel diameter matters more than extra length.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Why do curls fall out even with a good curling iron?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Curls usually fall because the sections are too large, the hair is not fully heated through, the curl is brushed too soon, or the barrel size is too large for the style. Letting each curl cool before touching it often matters as much as the tool itself.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Is a clip curling iron easier than a wand?</summary><p class="faq-answer">For most people, yes. A clip gives more control over the ends and makes repeatable curls easier. Wands are better for looser, undone texture, but they require more technique and carry more burn risk.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>What should I avoid if my hair is fine or damaged?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Avoid chasing 400–450°F heat, using oversized sections, and repeatedly passing over the same piece of hair. Fine or fragile hair needs controlled heat, smaller sections, and enough time for the curl to set without scorching it.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>How long should a good curling iron last?</summary><p class="faq-answer">A good curling iron should last several years with normal home use. Simple clip irons usually have fewer failure points than automatic curlers, but any hot tool should be replaced if heat becomes inconsistent, the clamp weakens, or the cord starts to wear.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>What are the signs a curling iron should be replaced?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Replace it if the heat cuts in and out, the clamp no longer holds evenly, the barrel surface feels sticky or scratched, the cord twists or frays, or you notice a burning smell. Those are safety and performance problems, not minor annoyances.</p></details>
</div>
</div>
